<section class="cs-section cs-section-surface" id="test">
  <h2>The same finding kept resurfacing across all three rounds of testing.</h2>
  <p class="cs-subtitle">Numbers and charts made the app feel cold. We had to learn to let it feel, instead of measure.</p>

  <p>It first showed up between low-fidelity and mid-fidelity: the first dashboard was full of charts and percentages, exactly what we were trying to keep the app away from. We simplified it into clear sections that move from the user's current state toward detail, not the other way around.</p>

  <div class="cs-visual-placeholder" style="aspect-ratio:16/9;">Dashboard before/after — from charts and percentages to a calmer, clearer read</div>

  <p>On the mid-fidelity prototype, tester Ambre surfaced the same finding from a different angle. Our stress-level slider ran on a 0 to 100 scale. Her reaction: people describe their state in colors, not numbers. "How many times did you feel red, how many times did you feel blue," as she put it, the same problem as the dashboard, in miniature: the app was measuring instead of listening. Mood colors used elsewhere in the app needed to hold everywhere, not just on some screens.</p>

  <p>On the finished high-fidelity prototype, we ran desirability testing to see if that shift had landed. People described the app in a few words of their own choosing: Friendly, Secure, Easy to use, not "efficient" or "clear," the words apps usually chase, but words that describe someone you trust, not a tool that grades you.</p>
</section>

<section class="cs-section" id="reflection">
  <h2>My role: brand, developing the SOS flow, and the tone in the app's most sensitive moments.</h2>
  <p class="cs-subtitle">What I take away from this project most isn't really about the design itself. It's about how we worked as a team.</p>

  <p>I owned the full visual identity on this project: the name Enow, the logo, and the whole brand style. The first idea for voice control and a bottom SOS button came from my teammate, and I took it from there, building it out into the SOS flow, onboarding, and the animations you see here.</p>

  <p>The biggest lesson came from how we organized as a team. We each work at a somewhat different pace, which isn't a problem on its own, but it demands more ongoing sharing and clearer rules from the start, for example around building and maintaining components in Figma.</p>

  <p>Given more time, I would have spent it tightening the timing on the animations and finishing the remaining exercise screens inside the SOS flow.</p>

  <p>You can find the full presentation and a click-through prototype linked at the top of this page.</p>
</section>
